[2026/05/16 16:14:19] [DEBUG] isgsa.backend/openai: [cyan]Raw func call response: Choice(finish_reason='tool_calls', index=0, logprobs=None, message=ChatCompletionMessage(content=None, refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=[], audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[ChatCompletionMessageFunctionToolCall(id='call_Dz2l3U5L6nrFhWzHlbgEdwVG', function=Function(arguments='{"nl_text":"I will build a compact, self-contained Python script that loads the MovieLens 100k u.data file, parses the user-item-rating-timestamp interactions, and computes the requested behavioral statistics directly from the full dataset. The script will confirm the number of unique users and items, identify the top 3 users by rating count, and compute the mean rating for the most active user. It will also compute the co-occurrence count for items 50 and 181 by counting users who rated both items. For timestamp sanity checking, it will compare observed timestamps against the known MovieLens 100k release-era range and report any values outside that window. I will use pandas and, where appropriate, lenskit-friendly data handling patterns, while keeping the implementation simple and runnable end-to-end. The script will also save intermediate experiment data, metrics, and any plottable arrays to the working directory using numpy save conventions. Finally, it will print the computed results clearly so the user can directly inspect the dataset properties without additional steps.","code":"import os\\nworking_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'working\')\\nos.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport pandas as pd\\n\\nexperiment_data = {\\n    \'ml100k_u_data\': {\\n        \'metrics\': {\'train\': [], \'val\': []},\\n        \'losses\': {\'train\': [], \'val\': []},\\n        \'predictions\': [],\\n        \'ground_truth\': [],\\n        \'timestamps\': [],\\n        \'counts\': []\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Locate the dataset\\ncandidate_paths = [\\n    os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'u.data\'),\\n    os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'ml-100k\', \'u.data\'),\\n    os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'data\', \'u.data\'),\\n    os.path.join(working_dir, \'u.data\'),\\n]\\ninput_path = None\\nfor p in candidate_paths:\\n    if os.path.exists(p):\\n        input_path = p\\n        break\\n\\nif input_path is None:\\n    raise FileNotFoundError(\'Could not locate MovieLens100k u.data in expected paths.\')\\n\\n# Load data\\nratings = pd.read_csv(\\n    input_path,\\n    sep=\'\\\\t\',\\n    header=None,\\n    names=[\'user_id\', \'item_id\', \'rating\', \'timestamp\'],\\n    engine=\'python\'\\n)\\n\\n# Core stats\\nunique_users = ratings[\'user_id\'].nunique()\\nunique_items = ratings[\'item_id\'].nunique()\\nuser_counts = ratings.groupby(\'user_id\').size().sort_values(ascending=False)\\ntop3_users = user_counts.head(3)\\nmost_active_user = int(top3_users.index[0])\\nmost_active_user_mean_rating = ratings.loc[ratings[\'user_id\'] == most_active_user, \'rating\'].mean()\\n\\n# Co-occurrence for item 50 and 181\\nusers_item_50 = set(ratings.loc[ratings[\'item_id\'] == 50, \'user_id\'])\\nusers_item_181 = set(ratings.loc[ratings[\'item_id\'] == 181, \'user_id\'])\\ncooccurrence_50_181 = len(users_item_50.intersection(users_item_181))\\n\\n# Timestamp sanity check for MovieLens 100k era\\n# Known timestamps are Unix seconds from late 1997 through early 1998.\\n# Use a conservative expected window: 1997-09-01 to 1998-04-30.\\nstart_ts = int(pd.Timestamp(\'1997-09-01\', tz=\'UTC\').timestamp())\\nend_ts = int(pd.Timestamp(\'1998-04-30 23:59:59\', tz=\'UTC\').timestamp())\\noutside_mask = (ratings[\'timestamp\'] < start_ts) | (ratings[\'timestamp\'] > end_ts)\\noutside_timestamps = ratings.loc[outside_mask, \'timestamp\']\\n\\n# Save plottable data / metrics\\nexperiment_data[\'ml100k_u_data\'][\'metrics\'][\'train\'].append({\\n    \'unique_users\': int(unique_users),\\n    \'unique_items\': int(unique_items),\\n    \'top1_user_id\': int(most_active_user),\\n    \'top1_user_rating_mean\': float(most_active_user_mean_rating),\\n    \'cooccurrence_50_181\': int(cooccurrence_50_181),\\n    \'timestamps_outside_expected_range\': int(outside_mask.sum())\\n})\\nexperiment_data[\'ml100k_u_data\'][\'counts\'] = user_counts.to_dict()\\nexperiment_data[\'ml100k_u_data\'][\'timestamps\'] = ratings[\'timestamp\'].to_numpy()\\n\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'experiment_data.npy\'), experiment_data)\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'ml100k_user_counts.npy\'), user_counts.to_numpy())\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'ml100k_user_counts_index.npy\'), user_counts.index.to_numpy())\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'ml100k_timestamps.npy\'), ratings[\'timestamp\'].to_numpy())\\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'ml100k_outside_timestamps.npy\'), outside_timestamps.to_numpy())\\n\\n# Print requested results\\nprint(f\'Loaded file: {input_path}\')\\nprint(f\'Unique users: {unique_users}\')\\nprint(f\'Unique items: {unique_items}\')\\nprint(\'Top 3 most active users by rating count:\')\\nfor uid, cnt in top3_users.items():\\n    print(f\'  user {int(uid)}: {int(cnt)} ratings\')\\nprint(f\'Most active user {most_active_user} mean rating: {most_active_user_mean_rating:.4f}\')\\nprint(f\'Users who rated both item 50 and item 181: {cooccurrence_50_181}\')\\nprint(f\'Timestamps outside expected 1997-09-01 to 1998-04-30 range: {int(outside_mask.sum())}\')\\nif outside_mask.any():\\n    print(f\'Outside-range timestamp min/max: {int(outside_timestamps.min())} / {int(outside_timestamps.max())}\')\\nelse:\\n    print(\'No timestamps outside the expected range were found.\')\\n"}', name='return_plan_and_code'), type='function')]))[/cyan]
